    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "When you were a child, did you already have a lot of trouble staying organized or keeping your attention on things?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, yeah, definitely. I mean, even in grade school, my teachers would always tell my mother that I was bright but just... scattered. I was always talking out of turn, losing my papers, needing constant reminders to finish my work. It was like I couldn't keep track of a single piece of paper to save my life. \n\nActually, speaking of losing things, just yesterday I lost my favorite pair of reading glasses. I swear I put them on the kitchen counter right next to the toaster, but when I went to look for them, they were just gone. I looked everywhere—under the couch, in the bathroom, even in the refrigerator! Can you believe that? I actually checked the fridge. I mean, sometimes I put the milk in the pantry, so I guess it’s not that crazy. But anyway, my neighbor, Mrs. Higgins, she has this theory that there are these little house gremlins that just take your things and hide them for fun. She’s a character, Mrs. Higgins. She makes these amazing snickerdoodle cookies, though. She brought some over last week when she came to complain about the mail carrier. He always leaves her packages on the bottom step instead of the porch... but wait, what were we talking about? Oh, right, school. Yeah, my teachers were always having to remind me to just stay on track and finish what I was doing.",
